{{define "title"}}{{.Area.Name}} — {{.T "site.title"}}{{end}}

{{define "main"}}
{{/* .frame, not .measure: this page carries a map and a chart, which need the
     data width. Slice D gives it the kit's .readouts and .map--wide; the
     container is what it needs now that <main> no longer clamps anything. */}}
<div class="frame">
<nav aria-label="breadcrumb"><a href="{{.Path "/"}}">{{.T "area.back_to_map"}}</a></nav>

{{/* The kit's .page-head: the name, then whatever qualifies it, above the
     figures rather than between them. */}}
<div class="page-head">
<h1 class="t-title">{{.Area.Name}}</h1>

{{/* Shown on city pages only, because this is the page where the misreading
     happens: a reader comparing two cities has no way to know that 14 of the
     27 are whole municipalities and the rest are the city proper. It is
     unconditional across cities rather than shown only for the 14, because
     nothing in the data marks which is which — the split is a property of
     OpenStreetMap's tagging, recorded in docs/known-limitations.md, not a
     column. Naming the counts keeps the sentence honest for both halves.

     Above the strip, not below it: it changes how every figure in the strip
     should be read, and a caveat printed after the numbers arrives too late. */}}
{{if eq .Area.Kind "city"}}
<p class="notice-inline">{{.T "area.city_boundary_note"}}
  <a href="{{.Path "/about-the-data"}}">{{.T "about.title"}}</a></p>
{{end}}
</div>

{{/* What this area is measuring right now — one cell per metric it reports,
     then the sensors behind them (PageData.AreaReadouts).

     The kit prints the sensor count twice, once as an intro sentence under the
     title and again as the fourth cell. Only the cell survives here: it is the
     same number, and the cell carries a tier line saying what the sensors are
     counted in, which the bare sentence did not. */}}
{{template "readouts-island" .}}

{{if not .Area.Covered}}
  <div class="notice">
    <p><strong>{{.T "area.no_coverage"}}</strong></p>
    <p>{{.T "area.no_coverage_detail"}}</p>
  </div>
{{end}}

{{/* Same toolbar as the map home, so the two screens put their controls in the
     same place. No spacer: this screen has nothing to sit opposite yet. */}}
<div class="toolbar">
<div data-island="switcher"
     data-metrics="{{.MetricsAttr}}"
     data-metric-labels="{{.MetricLabelsAttr}}"
     data-metric="{{.DefaultMetric}}"
     data-t-legend="{{.T "metric.legend"}}"></div>
</div>

{{if .Area.Covered}}
  {{/* The kit's sensor bar: which sensors the map draws, and how many there
       are. Not server-rendered — the counts come from the sensor payload the
       map fetches, and a filter with no JS is three radios that do nothing.

       Covered areas only: on an area with no recent readings every sensor is
       silent, so the filter's two other positions would offer the reader a
       choice between everything and nothing.

       The kit also puts a place-name search here. Left out: the sensors
       endpoint carries ids and coordinates, no place names. */}}
  <div data-island="sensorbar"
       data-metrics="{{.MetricsAttr}}"
       data-metric="{{.DefaultMetric}}"
       data-t-legend="{{.T "sensor.legend"}}"
       data-t-all="{{.T "sensor.all"}}"
       data-t-active="{{.T "sensor.active"}}"
       data-t-inactive="{{.T "sensor.inactive"}}"
       data-t-shown="{{.T "sensor.meta.shown"}}"
       data-t-of="{{.T "sensor.meta.of"}}"
       data-t-sensors="{{.T "sensor.meta.sensors"}}"
       data-t-silent="{{.T "sensor.meta.silent"}}"></div>
{{end}}

{{/* The shell is the positioning context for the on-map key, and it is a
     wrapper rather than the map itself because below 672px the kit moves the
     key out from over the map to under it — and inside .map, "under the map"
     is still over the map's own bottom corner. No .map--hero here, so the
     shell takes no width cap: the country fit is 926/382 and a province
     window is 926/560 (see --map-view-h in components.css). */}}
<div class="map-shell">
{{/* A map is a region with a name, not an unlabelled box: D4 found it
     announced only as "Map". The description points at the province list
     below, which is already the text alternative and the no-JS fallback,
     so the two are connected rather than merely both present. */}}
{{/* .map--wide is the kit's area-page map: full width of the frame, and a
     container-type so anything inside it can size itself against the map
     rather than against the viewport. */}}
<div id="area-map" data-island="map" class="data-frame map map--wide"
     role="region" aria-label="{{.T "map.label"}}"
     aria-describedby="area-map-alt"
     data-slug="{{.Area.Slug}}"
     data-zoom="{{.Area.Zoom}}"
     data-lon="{{.Area.Lon}}"
     data-lat="{{.Area.Lat}}"
     data-metric="{{.DefaultMetric}}"
     data-metrics="{{.MetricsAttr}}"
     data-metric-labels="{{.MetricLabelsAttr}}"
     data-metric-units="{{.MetricUnitsAttr}}"
     data-basemap="{{.BasemapStyleURL}}"
     data-lang-prefix="{{.LangPrefix}}"
     data-no-data-colour="{{.NoDataColour}}"
     data-unscaled-colour="{{.UnscaledColour}}"
     data-marker-stroke-colour="{{.MarkerStrokeColour}}"
     data-hex-opacity="{{.HexOpacity}}"
     data-marker-label-colour="{{.MarkerLabelColour}}"
     data-empty-basemap-colour="{{.EmptyBasemapColour}}"
     data-zoom-city="{{.ZoomCity}}"
     data-zoom-sensor="{{.ZoomSensor}}"
     data-t-legend="{{.T "map.legend.title"}}"
     data-t-legend-toggle="{{.T "map.legend.toggle"}}"
     data-t-legend-no-data="{{.T "map.legend.no_data"}}"
     data-t-legend-about="{{.T "map.legend.about"}}"
     data-t-legend-source="{{.T "map.legend.source"}}"
     data-t-disclaimer="{{.T "disclaimer"}}"
     data-t-close="{{.T "panel.close"}}"
     data-t-tier-country="{{.T "map.legend.tier.country"}}"
     data-t-tier-city="{{.T "map.legend.tier.city"}}"
     data-t-tier-sensors="{{.T "map.legend.tier.sensors"}}"
     data-t-fullscreen="{{.T "map.fullscreen"}}"
     data-t-fullscreen-exit="{{.T "map.fullscreen.exit"}}"
     data-t-zoom-in="{{.T "map.zoom.in"}}"
     data-t-zoom-out="{{.T "map.zoom.out"}}"
     data-t-zoom-reset="{{.T "map.zoom.reset"}}"
     {{template "mapLayerLabels" .}}
     data-t-hint="{{.T "map.hint.select_area"}}"
     data-t-rate-limited="{{.T "map.error.rate_limited"}}"
     data-t-unavailable="{{.T "map.error.unavailable"}}"
     data-t-unscaled="{{.T "metric.unscaled"}}"
     data-t-locate-button="{{.T "locate.button"}}"
     data-t-locate-denied="{{.T "locate.denied"}}"
     data-t-locate-failed="{{.T "locate.failed"}}"
     data-t-wind-toggle="{{.T "wind.toggle"}}"
     data-t-wind-about="{{.T "wind.about"}}"
     data-t-wind-note="{{.T "wind.note"}}"
     data-t-wind-attribution="{{.T "wind.attribution"}}"></div>
{{/* Same overlay as the home page's: the age of the data belongs beside the
     data, and this page's partial carries the reload button too. */}}
<div class="map-freshness">{{template "dataFreshness" .}}</div>
</div>
<p id="area-map-alt" class="visually-hidden">{{.T "map.text_alternative"}}</p>

{{/* #sensor= is honoured only here, never on index.gohtml: resolving it
     needs the sensor already loaded through the map island above (the
     registry in web/src/lib/sensors.svelte.js), and there is no
     /api/v1/sensor/{id} metadata endpoint to resolve one the map has not.
     On an area page the map's own slug is fixed (see the map island's data-
     slug above), so the sensor tier — and therefore this container's data —
     is reachable without a click, unlike on /. */}}
<div class="place-host" data-island="panel"
     data-metrics="{{.MetricsAttr}}"
     data-metric-labels="{{.MetricLabelsAttr}}"
     data-metric="{{.DefaultMetric}}"
     data-period="{{.DefaultPeriod}}"
     data-line-colour="{{.ChartLineColour}}"
     data-compare-colour="{{.ChartCompareColour}}"
     data-t-title="{{.T "panel.title"}}"
     data-t-close="{{.T "panel.close"}}"
     data-t-no-value="{{.T "panel.no_value"}}"
     data-t-flag-out-of-range="{{.T "panel.flag.out_of_range"}}"
     data-t-flag-stuck="{{.T "panel.flag.stuck"}}"
     data-t-flag-spatial-outlier="{{.T "panel.flag.spatial_outlier"}}"
     data-t-chart-title="{{.T "chart.title"}}"
     data-t-chart-value="{{.T "chart.axis.value"}}"
     data-t-chart-time="{{.T "chart.axis.time"}}"
     data-t-chart-empty="{{.T "chart.empty"}}"
     data-t-chart-unavailable="{{.T "map.error.unavailable"}}"
     data-periods="{{.PeriodsAttr}}"
     data-period-labels="{{.PeriodLabelsAttr}}"
     data-t-chart-metric-legend="{{.T "metric.legend"}}"
     data-t-chart-period-legend="{{.T "period.legend"}}"
     data-series-colours="{{.ChartSeriesColours}}"
     data-t-chart-reset="{{.T "chart.reset"}}"
     data-t-period-custom="{{.T "period.custom"}}"
     data-t-period-from="{{.T "period.from"}}"
     data-t-period-to="{{.T "period.to"}}"
     data-t-period-now="{{.T "period.now"}}"
     data-t-chart-range-invalid="{{.T "chart.range_invalid"}}"
     {{template "panelNearbyLabels" .}}
     data-t-details="{{.T "panel.details"}}"
     data-t-detail-devices="{{.T "panel.detail.devices"}}"
     data-t-detail-hardware="{{.T "panel.detail.hardware"}}"
     data-t-detail-since="{{.T "panel.detail.since"}}"
     data-t-detail-updated="{{.T "panel.detail.updated"}}"
     data-t-detail-coords="{{.T "panel.detail.coords"}}"></div>

{{if .Area.Covered}}
  {{/* The chart island, last on the page — after the map and the card the map
       opens. The kit reads top-down from "where" to "how much, over time", and
       a chart printed above the map answers a question the reader has not
       asked yet. It used to sit above the toolbar, which also put the metric
       switcher below the first thing that metric governs.

       Server-side we render nothing of the series itself —
       the numbers live behind the API so there is exactly one place that
       applies the quality filter — but the period vocabulary and the heading's
       parts are server data, so they arrive as attributes.

       data-periods is the config's own list (config.Series.PeriodNames), not a
       list written here: the API rejects any period outside it, so a hard-coded
       switcher is a button that returns 400 the day someone edits the config.
       The kit's mockup offers 6/12/24/42-hour windows, which describe a
       42-hour archive; this site keeps a year, so the kit's control is adopted
       and its options are read. */}}
  <div id="chart" data-island="chart"
       data-slug="{{.Area.Slug}}"
       data-metric="{{.DefaultMetric}}"
       data-period="{{.DefaultPeriod}}"
       data-periods="{{.PeriodsAttr}}"
       data-period-labels="{{.PeriodLabelsAttr}}"
       data-line-colour="{{.ChartLineColour}}"
       {{/* The three parts of the chart's heading. Composed in the browser
            rather than server-side, because the period part changes when the
            reader picks another window and a pre-composed sentence could not
            be rewritten. */}}
       data-t-metric="{{.T (printf "metric.%s" .DefaultMetric)}}"
       data-t-tier="{{.AreaTier}}"
       data-t-period-legend="{{.T "period.legend"}}"
       data-t-period-custom="{{.T "period.custom"}}"
       data-t-period-from="{{.T "period.from"}}"
       data-t-period-to="{{.T "period.to"}}"
       data-t-period-now="{{.T "period.now"}}"
       data-t-reset="{{.T "chart.reset"}}"
       data-t-range-invalid="{{.T "chart.range_invalid"}}"
       data-t-empty="{{.T "chart.empty"}}"
       data-t-value="{{.T "chart.axis.value"}}"
       data-t-unit="{{.T (printf "unit.%s" .DefaultMetric)}}"
       data-t-time="{{.T "chart.axis.time"}}"
       {{/* Reuses map.error.unavailable rather than adding a chart-specific
            key: the sentence is generic ("data is unavailable right now") and
            a second key would have to be translated, kept in parity across
            both catalogues, and would say the same thing. */}}
       data-t-unavailable="{{.T "map.error.unavailable"}}"></div>
{{end}}
</div>
{{end}}
